<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demetrius Zvonimir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1089">1089</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Dmitar Zvonimir · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimirovic-dynasty">Trpimirović dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demetrius Zvonimir was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> from 1076 to 1089, having earlier been ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>. He was crowned at Solin in 1076 by a legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>, swearing loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. His death without an heir led to a succession struggle in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-i-of-hungary">Ladislaus I of Hungary</a>, brother of his wife Helen, intervened, ending in the union of Croatia with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1102.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demetrius-zvonimir.jpg" alt="Demetrius Zvonimir" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Wein · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SplitBaptistryKing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zvonimir's origin is uncertain. Some historians believe he belonged to the native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimirovic-dynasty">Trpimirović dynasty</a>, while others reject this and suggest another prominent Croatian noble family. His early engagement to the Hungarian princess Helen, a daughter of Béla I, suggests an exceptional lineage. He served as ban under King Peter Krešimir IV, to whom he was related, and Peter Krešimir later appointed him duke and declared him heir. Around 1063 he sought protection from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> while the Croatian king faced a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and an attack by the Carantanian army of Ulric I. With help from Géza I and Solomon he restored authority in the March of Dalmatia between 1064 and 1067.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In March 1074 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> under Count Amico of Giovinazzo invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and captured a Croatian king, certainly Peter Krešimir, who died by November. Venice expelled the Normans in February 1075, and Zvonimir probably helped. Venice was close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and unfavourable to the Holy See, which drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and Zvonimir together. He was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a>, today Solin, in the Basilica of Saint Peter and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> by Gebizon, a representative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>, and took the forename Demetrius at the coronation. He swore fealty to the pope, pledged Peter's Pence and promised to support Church reform, which made the kingdom a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Holy See and ended its relations with Byzantium.</p><p>His reign is described as relatively peaceful, and he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a>. He tried to gain firmer control by replacing local nobles in the administration with court nobles, his own supporters and high clerics. Around 1079 the pope intervened on his behalf when Duke Vecelin prepared an attack from Istria, and in the 1080s Zvonimir sent naval troops to support the Norman invasion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Accounts of his death differ. The last document issued in his lifetime is dated 8 October 1087. Thomas the Archdeacon and a charter of 8 September 1089 indicate natural causes, which historians have mostly accepted since the sixteenth century, though some argued for a violent death. Later sources name 20 April and the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a>, and are tied to the allegation of assassination, now often considered a medieval legend.</p><p>He was married to Helen, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-i-of-hungary">Ladislaus I of Hungary</a>. Their son Radovan died before him, and they had two daughters, Vinica and Claudia. Without a descendant to succeed him, Zvonimir was followed by Stephen II, the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimirovic-dynasty">Trpimirović dynasty</a>, who ruled briefly until his death in 1091. Ten years of anarchy followed, and Ladislaus intervened, either invited by Croatian nobles or at the request of his sister.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Demetrius Zvonimir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Demetrius Zvonimir</b>, of the Svetoslavić branch of the House of Trpimirović, had married Helen of Hungary, sister of King Ladislaus I. After Zvonimir died in 1089 and his successor Stephen II died without an heir in 1091, civil war broke out.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Petar Krešimir IV (1058–1074) brought the Byzantine Dalmatian towns under his control, and <b>Demetrius Zvonimir</b> was crowned by a papal legate in 1076, tying Croatia to the Reformed papacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.03,16.18&amp;year=1079" aria-label="Knin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Demetrius Zvonimir"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4159 3761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4159 3761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.03,16.18&amp;year=1079">What was here in 1079? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
